The separation of hydrocarbons with carbon numbers in the 1–3 range is of great significance in the purification of natural gas, production of ethylene and propylene with the required $99.5\%+$ purity levels as feeds to polymerization reactors, and separation of ethyne from mixtures with ethylene. In this study the performances of a total of 19 different MOFs, carefully selected to span a wide range of functionalities, were evaluated for a variety of separation tasks. For all separation tasks investigated, MOFs with high density of open metal sites, CoMOF-74, $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ , and FeMOF-74, have significantly better separation potential than other MOFs, ZIFs or zeolites that have been suggested in the literature. Of particular significance is the possibility offered by CoMOF-74, MgMOF-74, and FeMOF-74 for ‘‘fractionating’’ a 6-component $\mathrm{CH_{4}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}/\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}/\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixture to yield individual pure components. Such separations are likely to have a significant industrial impact, resulting in significant energy savings when compared to current technologies that rely on distillation.
## Introduction
$\mathrm{C}_{1}$ to $\mathrm{C}_{3}$ light hydrocarbons, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ , ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ , and ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ , are very important energy resources and raw chemicals. For example, natural gas, whose main component is methane, has been considered as the most promising alternative fuel for future vehicle transportation; while both ethylene and propylene are important chemicals for the manufacture of polymers such as polyethylene, polypropylene, polyvinyl chloride, polyester, polystyrene as well as other organic chemicals. In order to fully utilize these light hydrocarbons, it is essential to have high quality and purity of such basic chemicals; thus, separations of these light hydrocarbons are very important industrial processes. The traditional separation technology of the cryogenic distillation, which is based on their different vapor pressures and thus boiling points, is very energy-intensive. Among several new
While $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture in pre- and post-combustion processing garners a lot of current research interest in the development of novel materials such as metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), there is a much wider scope for using MOF adsorbents in other processes of current and future industrial interest. The main focus of this article is to demonstrate the significant advantages of MOFs with open metal sites, for separation of light hydrocarbon mixtures, with C numbers in the 1–3 range, in a variety of contexts that are important in the petrochemical and energy sectors. Specifically, MgMOF-74, CoMOF-74, and FeMOF-74 have a strong propensity to bind, with high selectivity, alkynes and alkenes. This selective binding of double and triple bonds with the metal atoms leads to the possibility of separating ethylene–ethane, propylene–propane, and ethyne–ethylene mixtures, all of which are of great importance in the petrochemical industries. Furthermore, we demonstrate for the first time in the published literature the potential of $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ , CoMOF-74, and FeMOF-74 for fractionation of a 6-component $\mathrm{CH_{4}/C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixture, which is of great significance in natural gas processing.
energy-efficient technologies for such important hydrocarbon separations, adsorptive separation is one of the most promising ones, so a variety of microporous adsorbents have been examined for separations of these light hydrocarbons.
Microporous metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have been rapidly emerging as very useful materials for separations of small gas molecules.1–23 They can be readily self-assembled from metal ions and/or metal-containing clusters with organic linkers. Because the pores within such microporous MOFs can be tuned to maximize their size-selective sieving effects and the pore surfaces can be functionalized to direct their specific recognitions of small molecules, microporous MOFs are very unique materials for separations. In fact, a variety of microporous MOFs have been explored for their diverse gas separations such as separations of $\mathrm{CO_{2}}/\mathrm{CH_{4}},^{2,4,23-25}$ alkane and xylene isomers.26–34
Adsorptive separation of $\mathbf{C}_{1}$ methane from $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{3}$ hydrocarbons is comparatively easier given the fact that methane is the smallest molecule and therefore has weaker interactions with microporous MOF adsorbents.25,35–38 Separations of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ , and $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ , however, are very difficult, because these individual pair molecules have comparable sizes. In fact, only recently have a few microporous MOFs been targeted for these very challenging hydrocarbon separations.39–57 The four $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , and ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}{\bf\Sigma}$ ) and six ( $\mathrm{CH}_{4}.$ $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6},$ ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ , and ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ ) component separations are even much more challenging.43,58 Highly efficient separations of these mixtures of light hydrocarbons into their high-purity individual components can significantly save the energy cost, and thus are heavily in need. In this study, we examine the potential of microporous MOFs for adsorptive separations of these light hydrocarbons. For this purpose, 19 microporous MOFs of different pore structures, porosities, and surfaces have been selected to establish their hydrocarbon sorption isotherms, and to examine their potential for separations of light hydrocarbons through simulated breakthrough experiments. They have also been compared with the excellent performing microporous adsorbents, $\mathbf{NaX}$ ,59,60 NaETS-10,61 and FeMOF-74.43
## Results and discussions
## 1. MOF structures investigated for the light hydrocarbon separations
Specifically, the performances of various MOFs were evaluated for the following six different adsorptive separation tasks. (1) Separation of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , with $99\%+$ purity from an equimolar 4- component $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ mixture. (2) Investigating the possibility of ‘‘fractionating’’ a 4- component $\mathrm{CH_{4}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture to obtain each of the constituents in a nearly pure form. (3) Investigating the possibility of ‘‘fractionating’’ a 6- component $\mathrm{CH_{4}/C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixture to obtain each of the constituents in a nearly pure form. (4) Separation of an equimolar $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture for production of two products: (a) $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ of $99.5\%+$ purity
(as a feedstock for polymerization), and (b) ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{{H}}_{6}$ of $99\%+$   
purity. (5) Separation of an equimolar $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixture for   
production of two products: (a) ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ of $99.5\%+$ purity (as a   
feedstock for polymerization), and (b) ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ of $99\%+$ purity. (6) Selective removal of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ from a $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixture   
containing $1\%$ $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ to produce a $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ product containing   
${<}40\ \mathrm{ppm}\ C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$
Separations of these light hydrocarbons are conducted in Pressure Swing Adsorption (PSA) devices. It is well recognized that the separation characteristics of a PSA unit are dictated by a combination of adsorption selectivity and uptake capacity.74–76 Generally speaking, higher capacities are desirable because the adsorber bed can be run for a longer length of time before the need for regeneration arises. For a rational choice of adsorbents for mixture separation at high pressures, we need to have a proper method for evaluation that combines the selectivity and capacity metrics in a manner that is a true reflection of the separation performance of a PSA unit. For this purpose, we perform transient breakthrough calculations.76–78 To demonstrate the fractionation capability of MOFs with open metal sites, we also carry out pulse chromatographic simulations. The breakthrough simulation methodologies, with step- or pulseinputs of gas mixtures, are provided in the $\mathrm{ESI},\dag$ along with validations based on comparisons with available literature data.
## 2. Isosteric heat of adsorption
The binding energies of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ , ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ , and $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{8}}}$ in different MOFs are reflected in the isosteric heat of adsorption, $\varrho_{\mathrm{st}}^{\ }$ , defined as
$$
Q_{\mathrm{st}}=R T^{2}\bigg({\frac{\partial\ln p}{\partial T}}\bigg)_{\mathrm{q}}
$$
These values were determined using the pure-component isotherm fits. Fig. 2 presents data on the loading dependence of $-Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ of six different guest molecules in a small selection of the MOFs, which prove to be promising candidates in this study. The ability of any given structure to separate an alkene–alkane mixture is dependent, inter alia, on the differences in the adsorption strengths. A careful examination of the data in Fig. 2 reveals that for MOFs with open metal sites, CoMOF-74, $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ , and FeMOF-74, the values of $-Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ for alkenes are significantly higher than those of the corresponding alkanes with the same number of carbon atoms. For FeMOF-74, Bloch et al. have established that this is because each alkene molecule attaches side-on to an $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ atom within the FeMOF-74 framework.43 This large difference in the $-Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ values is the prime reason for the superior performance of MOF-74 in separating alkenes from alkanes as we shall demonstrate later in this article.
3. Evaluation of MOFs for separation of $\mathbf{CH_{4}}$ from a mixture of $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbons
Let us first consider the separation of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ from a mixture of $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbons. Rather than restricting our investigations to just binary $\mathrm{CH_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures, we consider the selective adsorption of $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbons from an equimolar 4-component $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture. The choice of such a mixture is dictated by the fact that such mixture separations are encountered in the process of oxidative coupling of methane for producing ethylene. The hierarchy of adsorption strengths is commonly $\mathrm{CH}_{4}<\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ $<\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}<\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , making the $\mathrm{CH_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation the crucial one. This implies that the conclusions drawn about the relative performance of different MOFs will hold equally well for the separation of a binary $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ mixture.
For the purpose of comparing different MOFs, we used two different metrics. The first metric is the adsorption selectivity, defined as
$$
S_{\mathrm{ads}}={\frac{q_{1}/q_{2}}{p_{1}/p_{2}}}
$$
that is determined using the Ideal Adsorbed Solution Theory (IAST) of Myers and Prausnitz.79 Here $p_{1}$ and $p_{2}$ are taken to be the partial pressures of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , respectively. $q_{1}$ and $q_{2}$ are the molar loadings in the adsorbed phase of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , respectively, expressed in moles per $\mathrm{kg}$ of the adsorbent material. The second metric of relevance is the uptake capacity. The separation performance of a 4-component $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}/$ ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ mixture will be dictated by the amount of combined total uptake of $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbons. Fig. 3 presents IAST calculations of the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}$ adsorption selectivity versus the uptake capacity of $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbons (combined total of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , and ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}{\bf\Sigma}$ ) for adsorption from an equimolar 4-component $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture at the total bulk gas phase at $296\mathrm{K}$ and 100 kPa. The uptake capacity is expressed in volumetric terms, i.e. as moles per litre of the adsorbent material. We observe from Fig. 3 that UTSA-34a has the highest selectivity, but a relatively low uptake capacity. CoMOF-74 and MgMOF-74 have the highest uptake capacities. Clearly, selectivity and capacity metrics do not appear to go hand-in-hand.
In order to properly evaluate the separation performance of a PSA unit, we performed breakthrough calculations with a stepinput of an equimolar 4-component $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ mixture. Fig. 4 presents data on the concentrations at the exit of the adsorber for six chosen MOFs, for illustration purposes. The $x$ -axis in Fig. 4 represents the dimensionless time, $\tau$ , defined by dividing the actual time, t, by the characteristic time, $\frac{L\varepsilon}{u}$ All adsorbents have the ability of separating $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in a pure form from this quaternary mixture. CoMOF-74 and $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ are of special interest because the breakthrough characteristics indicate the possibility that each of the individual components in the 4-component mixture can be obtained in its pure form.
From the data presented in Fig. 4, we can determine the $1\mathrm{mol\%}$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in the exit gas stream. Fig. 5 shows some typical plots of $1\mathrm{mol\%}$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in the exit gas stream as a function of the dimensionless time, $\tau$ , for a few typical MOFs. Assuming, arbitrarily, that a purity level of $99\%+$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is desired, we can determine the corresponding dimensionless time interval, $\Delta\tau$ . The amount of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , with the chosen $99\%+$ purity level, can be determined from a material balance over the adsorber by integrating the amount of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in the outlet gas during the time interval $\Delta\tau$ . Each MOF has a different value of $\Delta\tau$ during which $99\%+\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is available. Fig. 5 shows that the value of $\Delta\tau$ is highest for CoMOF-74, and it is to be expected that the production capacity of this MOF would also be the highest.
Fig. 6 presents a plot of $99\%+$ pure $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ produced, per litre of the adsorbent material, during the time interval, $\Delta\tau$ , plotted as a function of the time interval, $\Delta\tau$ . There is a reasonably good correlation between the productivity of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\Delta\tau$ ; this relationship is practically a linear one. From these results we note that the hierarchy of production capacities, per litre of the adsorbent, is CoMOF- $\mathbf{\cdot74}>\mathbf{MgMOF-74}>\mathbf{CuBTC}>\mathbf{MOF-505}$ $>$ UTSA-20. All other MOFs have significantly lower production capacities and are not viable adsorbents for use in practice. It is remarkable to note that this hierarchy is not dictated by the adsorption selectivity, but by a combination of adsorption selectivity and uptake capacity. Uptake capacities are of paramount importance in determining the performance of PSA units, as is evident from the fact that CoMOF-74 and MgMOF-74 have the best productivities, despite their only modest adsorption selectivities.
4. Adsorptive ‘‘fractionation’’ of $\mathbf{CH}_{4}I\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}I\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{4}I\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{6}$ mixtures
The breakthrough curves shown in Fig. 4 reveal some additional special features of CoMOF-74 and MgMOF-74 because the breakthrough times for each component, following the hierarchy $\mathrm{CH}_{4}<\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}<\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}<\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , are significantly different from each other. This implies that it may be possible to recover each component in a nearly pure form, which is a most desirable property. In order to further explore this property, we carried out pulse chromatographic simulations for separation of an equimolar 4-component $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ mixture. Fig. 7 presents the representative results obtained with (a) CoMOF-74, (b) $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ , (c) FeMOF-74, and (d) CuBTC. Of these materials, the breakthrough characteristics of CoMOF-74, $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ , and FeMOF-74 are remarkable. For these three materials, it appears that it is possible to obtain each individual component in its pure form. Such ‘‘fractionation’’ ability is not possessed by any of the other MOFs examined in this study. For example, with CuBTC, the peaks of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ overlap, signifying the fact that separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures is difficult with CuBTC as an adsorbent.
5. Adsorptive ‘‘fractionation’’ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}I$ $\mathbf{C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixtures
In the study of He et al.,58 it has been demonstrated that UTSA35a has the potential of separating an equimolar 6-component $\mathrm{CH_{4}/C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixture to obtain individual fractions based on the number of carbon atoms, i.e. $\mathbf{C}_{1}$ , $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{C}_{3}$ fractions. This ability of UTSA-35a to obtain individual $\mathbf{C}_{1}$ , $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{C}_{3}$ fractions is clearly demonstrated by the pulse chromatographic simulations for an equimolar 6-component mixture, see Fig. 8c. UTSA-35a is not capable of separating $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixtures, and the reason for this can be found in the
IAST calculations of the component loadings in Fig. 8a. We note that the adsorption loadings of ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}$ are practically identical in the whole range of pressures. A further implication is that the breakthrough times for ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{8}}}$ in a fixed bed adsorber are practically identical, see Fig. 8b. The IAST calculations also show that the component loadings of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ are close to each other. Consequently, the peaks of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ in the pulse simulations tend to overlap, see Fig. 8c.
MgMOF-74 and FeMOF-74 are much more potent. These two MOFs are capable of separating the 6-component mixture to obtain each of the components in a nearly pure form. The supporting data are presented in Fig. 9 and 10. These figures provide (a) IAST calculations, (b) breakthroughs in a fixed bed adsorber with step-inputs, and (c and d) pulse chromatographic simulations (two different scales are used in (c) and (d) for the same set of results to more clearly show the fractionation capabilities). The sequence of breakthroughs of the pulses in (c) and (d) of each component that emerge is dictated by the hierarchy in the component loadings in the IAST calculations.
According to our calculations (not shown here), CoMOF-74 has similar fractionation capability as $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ and FeMOF74. The US patent awarded to Matzger et al.56 presents pulse breakthrough experimental curves for separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixtures in fixed beds packed with CoMOF-74. These curves clearly demonstrate the fractionation capability of both binary mixtures. In the ESI,† pulse chromatographic simulation results for these mixtures are presented in order to demonstrate the agreement, albeit qualitatively, with the experimental results quoted in the patent.
## 6. Evaluation of MOFs for separation of $\mathbf{C_{2}H_{4}}/\mathbf{C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures
Let us consider the separation of binary $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures using MOFs that have a reasonable separation selectivity based on the information already obtained in the foregoing analysis of the different MOF structures. Separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures is industrially important in the context of production of polymergrade ethylene, whose purity needs to be $99.5\%+$ . Furthermore, for comparison purposes, the performances of $\mathbf{NaX}$ zeolite59 and NaETS- $\mathbf{10^{61}}$ were also evaluated. Fig. 11 shows the IAST calculations of the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ adsorption selectivity versus the volumetric uptake capacity of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ for adsorption from an equimolar $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture at the total bulk gas phase at
$296{\mathrm{~K~}}$ and $100~\mathrm{{kPa}}$ . The highest selectivity is obtained with NaETS-10, a material patented by Engelhard, but this material has only a limited uptake capacity. CoMOF-74 and MgMOF-74 have uptake capacities that are about twice as high as that for NaETS-10, albeit with lower selectivities.
For separations in a PSA adsorption device, we need to produce each of the two components in the mixture in a nearly pure form. The process occurs in two cycles. Let us first consider the adsorption cycle, in which $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ is selectively adsorbed. Fig. 12 shows transient breakthrough of an equimolar ethylene– ethane mixture in an adsorber bed packed with (a) CoMOF-74 and (b) $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ , chosen to illustrate just two of the several MOFs investigated. The inlet gas is maintained at partial pressures $p_{1}=p_{2}=50\mathrm{{kPa}}$ , at a temperature of $296\mathrm{K}$ . From the gas phase concentrations at the exit of the adsorber, we can determine the $1\mathrm{mol\%}$ ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ . This information is presented in Fig. 13. Let us arbitrarily choose the required purity of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ to be $99\%+$ . From Fig. 13, we can determine the time interval $\Delta\tau_{\mathrm{ads}}$ for each of the MOFs during which the purity level of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ is $99\%+$ . A material balance on the adsorber, obtained by integrating the amount of $99\%+$ pure ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ collected at the exit during this time interval $\Delta\tau_{\mathrm{ads}}$ , can be determined for each of the MOFs. Fig. 14 presents a plot of $99\%+$ pure ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ produced, per litre of the adsorbent material, during the time interval $\Delta\tau_{\mathrm{ads}},$ plotted as a function of the time interval, $\Delta\tau_{\mathrm{ads}}$ . There is a nearly perfect linear relation between the production of $99\%+$ pure ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and the time interval $\Delta\tau_{\mathrm{ads}}$ . From these results, we note that the hierarchy of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ production capacities, per litre of the adsorbent, is CoMOF- $\mathbf{\cdot}74>\mathbf{MgMOF-74}>\mathbf{CuBTC}>\mathbf{NaX}>\mathbf{NaETS-}$ ${\bf10}>{\bf P C N-16}>{\bf N O T T-102}>{\bf U T S A-20}.$ The high productivities of CoMOF-74 and $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ can be traced to the high uptake capacities.
Once the entire bed is in equilibrium with the partial pressures $p_{1}=p_{2}=50\mathrm{{kPa}}$ , at a temperature of $296\mathrm{K}$ , the desorption cycle is initiated, say with a purge (inert) gas. Fig. 15 illustrates transient breakthrough of an equimolar ethylene–ethane mixture in an adsorber bed packed with (a) CoMOF-74 and (b) MgMOF-74 in the desorption cycle of a PSA operation. During the desorption cycle, nearly pure $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ can be recovered. Fig. 16 shows mol $\%$ $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ in the outlet gas of an adsorber bed packed using three representative MOFs (CoMOF-74, MgMOF-74, and CuBTC) and NaETS-10 in the desorption cycle. For production of ethylene as a feedstock for polymerization purposes, the required purity level is $99.5\%+$ . From Fig. 16, we can determine the time interval, $\Delta\tau_{\mathrm{des}}$ , for each of the MOFs, during which the purity level of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ is $99.5\%+$ . A material balance on the adsorber, obtained by integrating the amount of $99.5\%+$ pure $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ collected at the exit during this time interval, $\Delta\tau_{\mathrm{des}}$ , can be determined for each of the MOFs. Fig. 17 presents a plot of $99.5\%+\$ pure $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ produced, per litre of the adsorbent, during the time interval, $\Delta\tau_{\mathrm{des}}$ , plotted as a function of the time interval, $\Delta\tau_{\mathrm{des}}$ . From these results we note that the hierarchy of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ production capacities, per litre of the adsorbent, is CoMOF- $^{74>}$ MgMOF- ${\bf74}>\mathrm{CuBTC}>\mathrm{NaX}>\mathrm{NaETS-10}>\mathrm{PCN-16}>\mathrm{NOT}$ T${\bf102}>$ UTSA-20. The good productivity of CoMOF-74 and $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ can be traced to the high uptake capacities. On the other hand, we note that the high selectivity of NaETS-10 for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation does not ensure its good productivity in the PSA adsorber because of its limited capacity.
The results presented in Fig. 14 and 17 can be combined to summarize the data on productivities of $99\%+$ pure ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ and $99.5\%+$ pure $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ by plotting the productivities against each other. For separation at a total pressure of $100\ \mathrm{kPa}$ and a temperature of $296\mathrm{K}$ , the data are summarized in the cross-plot presented in Fig. 18a; also included here are calculations for FeMOF-74 for which data are available at $318\mathrm{~K~}$ . FeMOF-74, CoMOF-74 and $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ clearly are the leading contenders for separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures. Fig. 18b presents a plot of the productivity of various MOFs for ethylene, expressed as a function of $S_{\mathrm{ads}}$ . We note that NaETS-10, with the highest value of $S_{\mathrm{ads}}$ , has a productivity that is only about a third of FeMOF74, CoMOF-74 and $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ .
## 7. Evaluation of MOFs for separation of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}/\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixtures
Let us consider the separation of binary $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixtures that is of importance in the context of production of polymergrade propylene, whose purity needs to be $99.5\%+$ . For comparison purposes, the performances of NaX zeolite60 and FeMIL- $\mathbf{100^{46}}$ were also evaluated. The calculation procedure is exactly analogous to that presented in the earlier section for ethylene–ethane mixtures. The final comparison results for the productivities of $99.5\%+$ pure ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ are presented in Fig. 19. FeMOF-74, CoMOF-74, and $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ have significantly higher productivities than other materials such as $\mathbf{NaX,^{60}}$ and $\mathbf{FeMIL-100^{46}}$ that have been suggested in the literature for this propylene production.
## 8. Evaluation of MOFs for separation of $\mathbf{C_{2}H_{2}}/\mathbf{C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures
In steam cracking of ethane to produce ethylene, one of the byproducts is ethyne. Ethyne has a deleterious effect on endproducts of ethylene, such as polyethylene. Therefore, recovery or removal of ethyne from ethylene streams is essential because the presence of ethyne at levels higher than $40\ \mathrm{ppm}$ will poison the catalyst. The selective removal of ethyne is conventionally carried out by absorption in DMF, which is energy-intensive. Selective adsorption is an energy-efficient alternative.
Let us consider the separation of a binary $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixture containing $1\%$ i.e. $10000\ \mathrm{ppm}$ , of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ in the mixture. Fig. 20 presents the IAST calculations of the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ adsorption selectivity versus the volumetric uptake capacity of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ for adsorption from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures containing $1\%\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . The highest adsorption selectivity is with M’MOF-3a, but its capacity for adsorption of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ is significantly lower than that of MgMOF-74 and CoMOF-74.
We now compare the separation characteristics of different MOFs for producing $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ containing less than $40\mathrm{ppm}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . As an illustration, Fig. 21 presents the transient breakthrough of a $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ feed mixture containing $1\%\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in an adsorber bed packed with (a) $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ and (b) CoMOF-74. From the concentrations at the outlet of the adsorber, we can determine the purity of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ in the exit gas stream. Fig. 22 presents a plot of the ppm $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the outlet gas of an adsorber bed packed with $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ , CoMOF-74, M’MOF-3a, and M’MOF-4a. The breakthrough times, $\tau_{\mathrm{break}}$ , corresponding to the exit gas containing 40 ppm $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ , can be determined. From a material balance on the adsorber, the production of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , with the specified purity level, can be determined by integrating during the time interval $0{-}\tau_{\mathrm{break}}$ . Fig. 23 presents a plot of the amount of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ produced, containing less than $40\mathrm{ppm}$ of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , per litre of the adsorbent, during the time interval $0{-}\tau_{\mathrm{break}}$ , plotted as a function of the time, $\tau_{\mathrm{break}}$ . The hierarchy of production capacities is MgMOF-74 > CoMOF-74 > M’MOF- $\mathbf{3a}>\mathbf{M}^{\bullet}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}{\mathbf{-}}\mathbf{4a}$ . The superior performance of $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ and CoMOF-74 can be traced to their high capacities to adsorb $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ . M’MOF- $\mathbf{\cdot3a}$ and M’MOF-4a have high selectivities but are subject to capacity limitations.
## Conclusions
Our study clearly demonstrates that microporous metal–organic frameworks are very promising materials for separations of light hydrocarbons. The separation capacities are dependent on both the selectivities and hydrocarbon uptakes of the microporous MOFs. Although tuning the pore sizes, curvatures, and porosities, to a certain extent, can adjust their separation capacities, the open metal sites still play the most important roles to differentiate their interactions with these light hydrocarbons. Exploration of new microporous MOFs with high density of open metal sites will be highly in need in the near future for the efficient separation of light hydrocarbons. More specifically, the following major conclusions can be drawn from this study.
(1) For separation of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , with $99\%+$ purity, from an equimolar $\mathrm{CH_{4}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture, the best materials are CoMOF-74 and MgMOF-74. (2) FeMOF-74, CoMOF-74, and MgMOF-74 are the best MOFs for separation of an equimolar $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture for production of two products: (a) $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ of $99.5\%+$ purity (as a feedstock for polymerization), and (b) ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ of $99\%+$ purity. (3) FeMOF-74, CoMOF-74, and MgMOF-74 are the best MOFs for separation of an equimolar $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixture for production of two products: (a) ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ of $99.5\%+$ purity (as a feedstock for polymerization), and (b) $\mathrm{{C_{3}H_{8}}}$ of $99\%+$ purity. (4) MgMOF-74, Co-MOF74, and M’MOF-3a are the three best materials for selective removal of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from a $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixture containing $10000\mathrm{ppm}{\mathrm C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ to produce a $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ product containing less than $40\mathrm{ppm}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . (5) MgMOF-74, FeMOF-74, and CoMOF-74 possess the ability of ‘‘fractionating’’ an equimolar 4-component $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture to obtain each of the constituents in a nearly pure form, and (6) $\mathbf{MgMOF-74}$ , FeMOF-74, and CoMOF-74 possess the ability of ‘‘fractionating’’ an equimolar 6-component $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixture to obtain each of the constituents in a nearly pure form.
The overall conclusion that can be drawn from our study is that FeMOF-74, CoMOF-74, and MgMOF-74 with high density of open metal sites are versatile adsorbents that can be used in all of the above-mentioned separation tasks, offering high production capacities per litre of adsorbent materials used in a PSA unit.
